(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["apis",{"c:messages-roles-and-system-prompts":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A chat model API takes an ordered list of \u003cb>messages\u003c/b>, each with a \u003cb>role\u003c/b> and some content, and returns the next assistant message plus a \u003cb>usage\u003c/b> record of tokens. The \u003cb>system\u003c/b> (or \u003cb>developer\u003c/b>) message sets the rules, \u003cb>user\u003c/b> and \u003cb>assistant\u003c/b> messages carry the conversation, and \u003cb>tool\u003c/b> messages carry the results of actions. The model keeps nothing between calls: the list you send \u003ci>is\u003c/i> the conversation.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The request, field by field\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Almost every provider and local server accepts this shape, called the \u003cb>Chat Completions\u003c/b> format after OpenAI's endpoint:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">json\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>{\n  \"model\": \"&lt;model&gt;\",\n  \"messages\": [\n    {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are Acme's support assistant. Answer in at most two sentences.\"},\n    {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"How do I reset my password?\"},\n    {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": \"Open Settings, then Security, and choose Reset password.\"},\n    {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"And if I no longer have access to that email address?\"}\n  ],\n  \"max_tokens\": 300,\n  \"temperature\": 0.2\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>model\u003c/code> picks the model. In production, pin a dated snapshot where the provider offers one, so behaviour does not shift under you.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>messages\u003c/code> is the whole conversation, oldest first. \u003ccode>content\u003c/code> is a string or a list of \u003cb>content parts\u003c/b> (text, image, file), which is how you send pictures and documents.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code> caps the output. It protects your bill and your latency. OpenAI's own API now prefers the name \u003ccode>max_completion_tokens\u003c/code> (required for its reasoning models); most other servers accept \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>temperature\u003c/code> controls randomness (the Inference module explains sampling).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>The roles\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Role\u003c/th>\u003cth>Written by\u003c/th>\u003cth>Used for\u003c/th>\u003cth>Provider notes\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>system / developer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your application\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Rules, persona, output format, background context\u003c/td>\u003ctd>OpenAI uses \u003ccode>developer\u003c/code> on newer models; Anthropic uses a top-level \u003ccode>system\u003c/code> field; Gemini uses \u003ccode>systemInstruction\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>user\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The end user, or your app on their behalf\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Questions, pasted text, uploaded files\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Treat as untrusted input\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>assistant\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The model, on earlier turns\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Its previous replies, which you send back\u003c/td>\u003ctd>You may also write one yourself, for example as a worked example\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>tool\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your code\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The result of a tool call the model asked for\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Anthropic puts a \u003ccode>tool_result\u003c/code> block inside a user message instead (Tools module)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>OpenAI documents a \u003cb>chain of command\u003c/b>: developer instructions outrank user messages when they conflict. All providers train their models to weight the system prompt heavily, but it is still just text in the same context window as the user's words.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The response\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">json\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>{\n  \"id\": \"chatcmpl-abc123\",\n  \"model\": \"&lt;model&gt;\",\n  \"choices\": [{\n    \"index\": 0,\n    \"message\": {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": \"You can verify your identity with a code sent to your phone instead.\"},\n    \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"\n  }],\n  \"usage\": {\n    \"prompt_tokens\": 81, \"completion_tokens\": 17, \"total_tokens\": 98,\n    \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\"cached_tokens\": 0},\n    \"completion_tokens_details\": {\"reasoning_tokens\": 0}\n  }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The two fields beginners skip and professionals always read:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>\u003ccode>finish_reason\u003c/code>\u003c/b> (Anthropic: \u003ccode>stop_reason\u003c/code>). \u003ccode>stop\u003c/code> (Anthropic \u003ccode>end_turn\u003c/code>) means the model finished. \u003ccode>length\u003c/code> (Anthropic \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code>) means the output was cut off at your limit. \u003ccode>tool_calls\u003c/code> (Anthropic \u003ccode>tool_use\u003c/code>) means the model wants you to run a tool. Anthropic also reports \u003ccode>refusal\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>pause_turn\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>\u003ccode>usage\u003c/code>\u003c/b>. Tokens in and out, plus details such as cached and reasoning tokens. This is your bill (Billing module).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>A multi-turn conversation in code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()  # reads OPENAI_API_KEY; base_url can point at any compatible server\nmessages = [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are a concise Python tutor. Answer in at most three sentences.\"}]\n\ndef ask(text: str) -&gt; str:\n    messages.append({\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": text})\n    r = client.chat.completions.create(model=\"&lt;model&gt;\", messages=messages, max_tokens=300)\n    reply = r.choices[0].message.content\n    messages.append({\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": reply})\n    print(f\"[{r.usage.prompt_tokens} in, {r.usage.completion_tokens} out, {r.choices[0].finish_reason}]\")\n    return reply\n\nask(\"What is a generator?\")\nask(\"Show me one in two lines.\")   # \"one\" only makes sense because turn 1 is resent\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Run it and watch \u003ccode>prompt_tokens\u003c/code> grow on every turn: each call resends the system prompt and the whole history. That growth drives the cost of long chats.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Keep system prompts in versioned files, not string literals scattered through code (Prompting module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Put stable content first and changing content last; it makes prompt caching work (Billing module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Check \u003ccode>finish_reason\u003c/code> before using the output. Never parse truncated JSON as if it were complete.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Never put secrets in a prompt. Models can be talked into repeating anything in their context.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The system prompt is not a security boundary. Enforce permissions in code, not in instructions (Security module).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you build a multi-turn conversation with a system prompt?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Keep a list that starts with the system (or developer) message, append each user message, send the whole list on every call, and append the assistant's reply to the list before the next user turn. With Anthropic you pass the system text in the top-level \u003ccode>system\u003c/code> field and only user and assistant turns in \u003ccode>messages\u003c/code>. The model \"remembers\" earlier turns only because you resend them each time.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A response comes back with \u003ccode>finish_reason: \"length\"\u003c/code>. What happened and what do you do?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The output hit your \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code> limit and was cut off mid-answer. Raise the limit, ask for shorter output, or continue in a follow-up turn; do not treat the text (especially JSON) as complete.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is the system prompt not a security boundary?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It is text in the same context as user input and retrieved documents. A crafted message can persuade the model to ignore it (prompt injection), so anything that must never happen has to be blocked by code and permissions.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:conversation-state-and-the-responses-api":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Chat Completions and Anthropic's Messages API are \u003cb>stateless\u003c/b>: you send the full history on every call. OpenAI's Responses API and Google's Interactions API can also keep conversation state on the server, so you send only the new message plus an id. Server-side state saves bandwidth and code, \u003cb>not tokens\u003c/b>: the model still reads, and you still pay for, the whole history on every turn.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Stateless: you own the history\u003c/h4>\u003cp>With a stateless API your application stores the conversation (in memory, a database or a cache) and sends it each time, exactly as in the previous lesson. That sounds wasteful, but it gives you complete control:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>You can \u003cb>edit\u003c/b> history: trim old turns, summarise them, remove a tool result that is no longer needed (Memory module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>It is \u003cb>portable\u003c/b>: the same history can go to another provider or a local model.\u003c/li>\u003cli>It is \u003cb>easy to debug and evaluate\u003c/b>: you can replay any exact request from your logs.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Nothing is stored at the provider beyond its standard retention, which matters for privacy rules.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>The cost is a growing payload and the job of storing it yourself.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Stateful: the provider owns the history\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>API (as of late 2026)\u003c/th>\u003cth>How you continue\u003c/th>\u003cth>What the provider stores\u003c/th>\u003cth>Notes\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>OpenAI Responses API\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Pass \u003ccode>previous_response_id\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Each response object, 30 days by default\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Set \u003ccode>store=false\u003c/code> to store nothing and send history yourself\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>OpenAI Conversations API\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Pass \u003ccode>conversation\u003c/code> with a conversation id\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A durable conversation object and its items\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Items are not subject to the 30-day limit\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Gemini Interactions API\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Pass \u003ccode>previous_interaction_id\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Interactions, by default\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>store=false\u003c/code> switches to stateless mode\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Anthropic Messages API\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Resend the messages\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Nothing; the API is stateless\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Anthropic's hosted agent products manage sessions for you (Agents stage)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Here is the Responses API version of a two-turn chat:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()\nrules = \"You are a concise Python tutor. Answer in at most three sentences.\"\n\nr1 = client.responses.create(model=\"&lt;model&gt;\", instructions=rules, input=\"What is a generator?\")\nr2 = client.responses.create(model=\"&lt;model&gt;\", instructions=rules,\n                             previous_response_id=r1.id, input=\"Show me one in two lines.\")\nprint(r2.output_text)\nprint(r2.usage.input_tokens)   # includes turn 1 and its answer: the whole chain is billed\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Two details from the API reference trip people up. First, \u003ccode>instructions\u003c/code> from the previous response are \u003cb>not carried over\u003c/b>, so pass them on every call (this lets you change them between turns). Second, OpenAI's guide states plainly that even with \u003ccode>previous_response_id\u003c/code>, all previous input tokens in the chain are billed as input tokens.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What server-side state really buys you\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Less code and bandwidth.\u003c/b> You send a short request instead of a long one; useful on mobile clients and for very long histories with images.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reasoning carried across turns.\u003c/b> Reasoning models produce hidden reasoning items. Chained responses pass them along automatically. In stateless mode (\u003ccode>store=false\u003c/code> or zero data retention) the response includes these items in encrypted form and you must send them back yourself to get the same effect.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Built-in tools and compaction.\u003c/b> Hosted tools and server-side compaction (Memory module) work most smoothly when the provider holds the state.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>And what it costs you:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Lock-in.\u003c/b> A conversation stored at one provider cannot be continued at another.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Less control.\u003c/b> You cannot easily edit the middle of a chain; you fork from an earlier response instead.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Retention.\u003c/b> Stored responses and conversations are data held by a third party; check this against your privacy commitments.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Decision guide\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Situation\u003c/th>\u003cth>Choose\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>You need to trim, summarise or redact history\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Stateless\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Multi-provider routing or fallback\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Stateless\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Strict data retention or zero data retention\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Stateless (\u003ccode>store=false\u003c/code>)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Simple chat on one provider, long histories, mobile clients\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Stateful is convenient\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Reasoning models with tools on OpenAI\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Either: chain responses, or pass reasoning items back\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Many teams use stateless calls in their backend and keep their own conversation store as the source of truth, even on providers that offer state, because their evals, logs and memory features need the exact history anyway.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you continue a conversation with stored state versus resending history, and what is the difference in cost?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Resending history: keep the message list yourself and send all of it on every call (Chat Completions, Anthropic Messages, or Responses with \u003ccode>store=false\u003c/code>). Stored state: send only the new user input plus \u003ccode>previous_response_id\u003c/code> (OpenAI Responses), a \u003ccode>conversation\u003c/code> id (OpenAI Conversations API) or \u003ccode>previous_interaction_id\u003c/code> (Gemini Interactions). The token bill is essentially the same either way, because the model still processes the whole history and the chained input is billed; you save bandwidth and code, not tokens.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You chain responses with \u003ccode>previous_response_id\u003c/code> and the assistant suddenly ignores its persona on turn 2. What is the likely bug?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The \u003ccode>instructions\u003c/code> were passed only on the first call. They are not carried over when chaining, so they must be sent with every request.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Give two reasons to keep your own conversation store even on a provider that offers server-side state.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Any two of: you can edit, trim or summarise history; you can switch or fall back to another provider; you can replay exact requests for debugging and evals; you control retention and deletion of user data.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:streaming-responses":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>With \u003ccode>stream: true\u003c/code> the API sends the answer as it is generated, as a series of \u003cb>server-sent events\u003c/b> (SSE) over one long HTTP response. Users see the first words after the \u003cb>time to first token\u003c/b> (TTFT) instead of waiting for the whole answer. Your client must assemble the fragments (\u003cb>deltas\u003c/b>), pick up usage at the end, and decide what to do when a stream breaks halfway.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What SSE looks like on the wire\u003c/h4>\u003cp>SSE is a simple text protocol: the response has \u003ccode>Content-Type: text/event-stream\u003c/code>, and each event is one or more \u003ccode>field: value\u003c/code> lines followed by a blank line. Here is a raw Chat Completions stream (ids shortened):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>data: {\"id\":\"c1\",\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"delta\":{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"\"}}]}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"c1\",\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"delta\":{\"content\":\"Gener\"}}]}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"c1\",\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"delta\":{\"content\":\"ators yield\"}}]}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"c1\",\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"delta\":{},\"finish_reason\":\"stop\"}]}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"c1\",\"choices\":[],\"usage\":{\"prompt_tokens\":25,\"completion_tokens\":61,\"total_tokens\":86}}\n\ndata: [DONE]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Each \u003ccode>delta\u003c/code> holds only the new text. The usage chunk appears only if you ask for it with \u003ccode>stream_options: {\"include_usage\": true}\u003c/code>; without it you get no token counts from a streamed call. \u003ccode>[DONE]\u003c/code> ends the stream.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Other APIs use \u003cb>named events\u003c/b> with an \u003ccode>event:\u003c/code> line:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>API\u003c/th>\u003cth>Main events\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>OpenAI Responses\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>response.created\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>response.output_text.delta\u003c/code> (many), \u003ccode>response.completed\u003c/code> (carries usage), \u003ccode>error\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Anthropic Messages\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>message_start\u003c/code> (input usage), \u003ccode>content_block_start\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>content_block_delta\u003c/code> (many), \u003ccode>content_block_stop\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>message_delta\u003c/code> (stop reason, cumulative output usage), \u003ccode>message_stop\u003c/code>, plus \u003ccode>ping\u003c/code> keep-alives\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Tool calls arrive in pieces too\u003c/h4>\u003cp>When the model calls a tool while streaming, its arguments arrive as \u003cb>fragments of a JSON string\u003c/b>: in Chat Completions as \u003ccode>delta.tool_calls[i].function.arguments\u003c/code>, in Anthropic as \u003ccode>input_json_delta\u003c/code> events with a \u003ccode>partial_json\u003c/code> field. Accumulate the fragments per tool-call index and parse the JSON only when that block ends. Parsing early fails on strings like \u003ccode>{\"city\": \"Par\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Stream to a terminal and measure TTFT\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import time\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(timeout=60)\nt0 = time.perf_counter()\nttft, parts, usage = None, [], None\n\nstream = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=\"&lt;model&gt;\",\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Explain Python generators in about 150 words.\"}],\n    stream=True,\n    stream_options={\"include_usage\": True},\n)\nfor chunk in stream:\n    if chunk.choices and chunk.choices[0].delta.content:\n        if ttft is None:\n            ttft = time.perf_counter() - t0          # first visible token\n        parts.append(chunk.choices[0].delta.content)\n        print(chunk.choices[0].delta.content, end=\"\", flush=True)\n    if chunk.usage:\n        usage = chunk.usage\n\ntotal = time.perf_counter() - t0\nspeed = usage.completion_tokens / (total - ttft)\nprint(f\"\\nTTFT {ttft:.2f}s | total {total:.2f}s | {speed:.0f} output tokens/s\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Three numbers matter: \u003cb>TTFT\u003c/b> (how long users stare at a spinner), \u003cb>total time\u003c/b>, and \u003cb>output speed\u003c/b> in tokens per second. With a reasoning model, TTFT includes all the hidden thinking, so it can be many seconds even though the stream works perfectly. The tokens-per-second figure for such a model is also an underestimate, because \u003ccode>completion_tokens\u003c/code> includes reasoning tokens produced before the first visible one.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When the stream breaks\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A streamed request returns HTTP 200 as soon as it starts, so errors that happen later (for example an \u003ccode>overloaded_error\u003c/code> from Anthropic) arrive \u003cb>as events inside the stream\u003c/b>, not as an HTTP status. Connections also drop. Your options:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Nothing shown yet:\u003c/b> retry the whole request with backoff, as for any error.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Text already shown:\u003c/b> do not silently replay the request; the user would see a different answer appear. Show what you have, mark it interrupted, and offer a retry, or ask the model to continue. Anthropic's guide shows the pattern for its newer models: a new request whose user message contains the partial answer and asks the model to continue from where it stopped. Tool-call and thinking blocks cannot be partly recovered; only text can.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Idle stream:\u003c/b> set a read timeout (for example 30 to 60 seconds without a byte) so a stalled connection fails instead of hanging. Anthropic sends \u003ccode>ping\u003c/code> events partly so idle connections stay alive.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Stream every user-facing response longer than a sentence. It does not make generation faster, but perceived speed improves a lot.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If you relay the stream through your own server to a browser, make sure proxies do not buffer it (nginx buffers by default; send the \u003ccode>X-Accel-Buffering: no\u003c/code> header or disable \u003ccode>proxy_buffering\u003c/code>), and close the upstream stream when the browser disconnects.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Moderating streamed output is harder because you see it in pieces; OpenAI's guide flags this for production use.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Log TTFT and total time per request; they are the two latency numbers you will be asked about (Reliability module).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you stream tokens to a terminal and measure time to first token?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Send the request with \u003ccode>stream=True\u003c/code> (and \u003ccode>stream_options={\"include_usage\": True}\u003c/code> for token counts), iterate over the chunks, and print each \u003ccode>delta.content\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>flush=True\u003c/code>. Record a timestamp just before the call and another at the first chunk that carries non-empty content: the difference is TTFT. Record the total at the end, and compute output speed as output tokens divided by (total time minus TTFT).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why might a streamed response contain an error even though the HTTP status was 200?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The status is sent when the stream starts. Problems that happen during generation, such as overload, are delivered as error events inside the stream, so the client must check event types, not just the status code.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your code calls \u003ccode>json.loads\u003c/code> on each tool-call delta and crashes. Why, and what is the fix?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Tool arguments stream as fragments of one JSON string, so each fragment alone is invalid JSON. Concatenate the fragments for each tool call and parse once the block (or the whole message) is complete.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:rate-limits-retries-and-backoff":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Model APIs fail often enough that every production client needs a policy: \u003cb>classify\u003c/b> the error, \u003cb>retry\u003c/b> only transient ones, wait with \u003cb>exponential backoff plus random jitter\u003c/b> (honouring \u003ccode>Retry-After\u003c/code>), and bound everything with \u003cb>timeouts\u003c/b>. Retrying the wrong errors, or retrying in lockstep, turns a small blip into an outage and a bigger bill.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Know your error classes\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Status\u003c/th>\u003cth>Typical meaning\u003c/th>\u003cth>Retry?\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>400\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Malformed request, unknown parameter, prompt too long for the context window (Anthropic also returns 400 when a spend limit you set is reached)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No: fix the request\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>401, 403\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Bad, revoked or expired key; key lacks permission; unsupported region or blocked IP\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>404\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Wrong model name or endpoint\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>413\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Request body too large (Anthropic: 32 MB for Messages)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No: shrink it\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>429 rate limit\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Too many requests or tokens per minute, or traffic ramped up too fast (OpenAI code \u003ccode>slow_down\u003c/code>)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes, after \u003ccode>Retry-After\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>429 billing\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Credits exhausted or spend limit reached (OpenAI codes such as \u003ccode>credit_balance_exhausted\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>project_spend_limit_exceeded\u003c/code>; Anthropic's spend-cap 429 has no \u003ccode>retry-after\u003c/code>)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No: alert a human\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>500\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Provider bug\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes, with backoff\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>503 / 529\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Model overloaded (OpenAI 503 \u003ccode>server_is_overloaded\u003c/code>, Anthropic 529 \u003ccode>overloaded_error\u003c/code>)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes; consider a fallback model\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>504\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Request took too long on the server\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes; consider streaming\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>No response\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Connection reset, DNS failure, your own timeout\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The key subtlety: \u003cb>one status code can mean both a retryable and a permanent problem\u003c/b>. Always inspect the error body (\u003ccode>error.type\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>error.code\u003c/code>) as well as the status. And save the \u003cb>request id\u003c/b> (\u003ccode>x-request-id\u003c/code> at OpenAI, \u003ccode>request-id\u003c/code> at Anthropic) in your logs; support will ask for it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Exponential backoff with full jitter\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Backoff\u003c/b> means waiting longer after each failure: 1, 2, 4, 8 seconds. \u003cb>Jitter\u003c/b> means randomising each wait. Without jitter, a thousand clients that failed in the same second all retry at exactly 1 s, 2 s and 4 s later, hitting the provider in synchronised waves (the \"thundering herd\"). \u003cb>Full jitter\u003c/b> picks a random wait between zero and the backoff ceiling:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Attempt that failed\u003c/th>\u003cth>Ceiling (base 1 s, cap 30 s)\u003c/th>\u003cth>Actual wait\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>1\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1 s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>random 0 to 1 s\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2 s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>random 0 to 2 s\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>3\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4 s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>random 0 to 4 s\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>4\u003c/td>\u003ctd>8 s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>random 0 to 8 s\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>5\u003c/td>\u003ctd>16 s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>give up and surface the error\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>If the server sends \u003ccode>Retry-After\u003c/code>, wait at least that long.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import random\nimport time\n\nimport httpx\nimport openai\n\nclient = openai.OpenAI(timeout=httpx.Timeout(60.0, connect=5.0), max_retries=0)  # we own the policy\n\nRETRYABLE = (openai.APIConnectionError,   # includes APITimeoutError\n             openai.RateLimitError,       # 429\n             openai.InternalServerError)  # 5xx, including 503 overloaded\nNEVER_RETRY = {\"insufficient_quota\", \"credit_balance_exhausted\",\n               \"organization_spend_limit_exceeded\", \"project_spend_limit_exceeded\"}\n\ndef retry_after(e) -&gt; float:\n    headers = getattr(getattr(e, \"response\", None), \"headers\", None) or {}\n    try:\n        return float(headers.get(\"retry-after\", 0))\n    except ValueError:\n        return 0.0\n\ndef create_with_retries(max_attempts=5, base=1.0, cap=30.0, **kwargs):\n    for attempt in range(1, max_attempts + 1):\n        try:\n            return client.chat.completions.create(**kwargs)\n        except RETRYABLE as e:\n            if getattr(e, \"code\", None) in NEVER_RETRY or attempt == max_attempts:\n                raise\n            ceiling = min(cap, base * 2 ** (attempt - 1))\n            time.sleep(max(random.uniform(0, ceiling), retry_after(e)))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Note \u003ccode>max_retries=0\u003c/code>. The official OpenAI and Anthropic Python SDKs already retry connection errors, 429s and 5xx twice by default with backoff. That is fine on its own, but if you add your own loop, switch the SDK's off, or five attempts each doing three tries becomes fifteen calls.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Timeouts per phase\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A single \"timeout\" hides several different waits. Set each one deliberately:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Phase\u003c/th>\u003cth>What it bounds\u003c/th>\u003cth>Typical setting\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Connect\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Opening the TCP and TLS connection\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2 to 5 s\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Read (idle)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Silence between bytes; for streams, between events\u003c/td>\u003ctd>30 to 60 s; longer for reasoning models that think before the first token\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Request\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One whole attempt\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Expected time plus margin: 1,000 output tokens at 50 tokens/s is about 20 s, plus TTFT\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Overall deadline\u003c/td>\u003ctd>All attempts plus waits, as the user experiences it\u003c/td>\u003ctd>For interactive chat often 30 to 60 s; for background jobs, minutes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The SDKs' default timeout is ten minutes, which is reasonable for a batch script and far too long for a web request. Pass your own (\u003ccode>httpx.Timeout(60.0, connect=5.0)\u003c/code> above) and check the remaining deadline before each retry, so retries never outlive the user's patience.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Idempotency: what retrying can repeat\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A model call has no side effects, so retrying it is safe, but not free: a request that timed out on your side may still have completed (and been billed) on the provider's side, and the retry may produce a different answer. The danger is in \u003cb>tools\u003c/b>. If an agent's \u003ccode>create_ticket\u003c/code> call times out and you retry, you may create two tickets. Give side-effecting tools an \u003cb>idempotency key\u003c/b> (a unique id per intended action, which the downstream API uses to ignore duplicates) and make the tool check it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Retry inside a \u003cb>budget\u003c/b>: cap retries at a small share of traffic (for example 10%) so a provider incident does not double your load. Gateways and circuit breakers do this centrally (Reliability module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>After a few failures on one model, \u003cb>fall back\u003c/b> to another model or provider instead of hammering the same one.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For a streamed response, retry automatically only if no text has been shown yet.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Log every attempt: status, error type and code, request id, wait time. Error-rate dashboards are built from these lines.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you implement exponential backoff with jitter?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Wrap the call in a loop over a maximum number of attempts. Catch only retryable errors (connection errors and timeouts, rate-limit 429s, 5xx and overload). After failure number n, sleep a random time between 0 and min(cap, base × 2^(n-1)), or the \u003ccode>Retry-After\u003c/code> value if that is larger, then try again; re-raise on non-retryable errors or after the last attempt. Disable the SDK's built-in retries so the two policies do not multiply.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which errors should you never retry automatically, and why?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>400 (bad request), 401 and 403 (authentication and permission), 404 (wrong model or endpoint), 413 (too large) and billing or quota 429s such as exhausted credits or a reached spend limit. The same request will fail again, so retrying only adds load, delay and log noise; fix the request or alert a human.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does jitter matter if every client already uses exponential backoff?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Without randomness, clients that failed together retry together, producing synchronised waves of load that keep the provider overloaded. Random waits spread the retries out.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">An agent's \"create ticket\" tool call timed out and your loop retried it. What could go wrong and how do you prevent it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The first call may have succeeded after your timeout, so the retry creates a duplicate ticket. Send an idempotency key with each intended action and have the tool or downstream API ignore repeats with the same key.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:apis/rate-limits-and-throttling":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Providers cap how much you can send per minute, separately for \u003cb>requests\u003c/b> (RPM) and \u003cb>tokens\u003c/b> (TPM, or input and output tokens separately at Anthropic). Limits are set per organisation or project and per model, rise with your spending tier, and are enforced continuously like a \u003cb>token bucket\u003c/b>. You will usually hit the token limit before the request limit, so plan capacity in tokens and throttle on your side before the provider does it for you.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The metrics\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Metric\u003c/th>\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>RPM / RPD\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Requests per minute / per day\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>TPM / TPD\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tokens per minute / per day (OpenAI counts input plus output)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>ITPM / OTPM\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Input and output tokens per minute, counted separately (Anthropic)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Batch queue limit\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tokens you may have waiting in batch jobs (separate pool)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Limits are shared by everything that uses the same organisation or project, which is why two services that are each \"well under the limit\" can still collide. Tiers rise automatically with spending: as of late 2026 OpenAI's paid tiers are Build, Launch and Grow, unlocked at $5, $100 and $500 of total credit purchases. Anthropic says it uses the \u003cb>token bucket algorithm\u003c/b>: capacity refills continuously up to your maximum rather than resetting once a minute, so short bursts are fine but a sustained rate above the limit is not.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How tokens are counted differs\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Two provider details change your capacity planning:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Output reservations.\u003c/b> OpenAI's guide says the limiter counts your request by its estimated size and your \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code>, whichever is larger, so an oversized \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code> uses up headroom you never use. Set it close to the real output size. Anthropic counts output tokens as they are generated, so a large \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code> costs no rate-limit headroom there.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cached tokens.\u003c/b> For most Claude models, tokens read from the prompt cache do \u003cb>not\u003c/b> count toward ITPM; Anthropic's example is a 2,000,000 ITPM limit with an 80% cache hit rate handling 10,000,000 total input tokens a minute. At OpenAI, cached tokens still count toward TPM.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Worked example: what is my real throughput?\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Your limits for one model: 1,000 RPM and 450,000 input tokens per minute. A typical request has 6,000 input tokens.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Token-bound rate:   450,000 / 6,000 = 75 requests per minute\nRequest-bound rate: 1,000 requests per minute\nReal ceiling:       75 requests per minute (the smaller one)\n\nIf 75% of each prompt is read from cache and cache reads are exempt (most Claude models):\nuncached input per request = 6,000 × 0.25 = 1,500\ntoken-bound rate = 450,000 / 1,500 = 300 requests per minute\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The same arithmetic tells you when to ask for a higher tier, when to shorten prompts, and how many concurrent users a feature can serve.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Read the headers\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every response tells you where you stand, so you can slow down before you get a 429:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>OpenAI\u003c/th>\u003cth>Anthropic\u003c/th>\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>x-ratelimit-remaining-requests\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>anthropic-ratelimit-requests-remaining\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Requests left in the window\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>x-ratelimit-remaining-tokens\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>anthropic-ratelimit-input-tokens-remaining\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>...-output-tokens-remaining\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tokens left\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>x-ratelimit-reset-requests\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>x-ratelimit-reset-tokens\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>anthropic-ratelimit-requests-reset\u003c/code> and similar (RFC 3339 time)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>When capacity is back\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>Retry-After\u003c/code> (on some 429 and 503 responses)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>retry-after\u003c/code> (on most 429s)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Seconds to wait\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Throttle on your side: a token bucket\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A client-side limiter keeps you just under the provider's limit, so requests queue in your process instead of failing. This asyncio version allows a fixed number of tokens per minute, refilled continuously:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import asyncio\nimport time\n\nclass TokenBucket:\n    \"\"\"Allow `per_minute` units per minute, refilled continuously.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, per_minute: int):\n        self.capacity = per_minute\n        self.level = float(per_minute)\n        self.rate = per_minute / 60.0           # units per second\n        self.updated = time.monotonic()\n        self.lock = asyncio.Lock()\n\n    async def take(self, n: int) -&gt; None:\n        assert n &lt;= self.capacity, \"a single request larger than the bucket can never pass\"\n        async with self.lock:                   # waiters are served one at a time\n            while True:\n                now = time.monotonic()\n                self.level = min(self.capacity, self.level + (now - self.updated) * self.rate)\n                self.updated = now\n                if self.level &gt;= n:\n                    self.level -= n\n                    return\n                await asyncio.sleep((n - self.level) / self.rate)\n\ntpm = TokenBucket(400_000)   # stay ~10% under a 450,000 limit\nrpm = TokenBucket(900)\nconcurrency = asyncio.Semaphore(20)\n\nasync def limited_call(messages, max_tokens=500):\n    estimate = sum(len(m[\"content\"]) for m in messages) // 4 + max_tokens   # ~4 characters per token\n    await rpm.take(1)\n    await tpm.take(estimate)\n    async with concurrency:\n        ...  # await the API call here\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Three controls work together: the \u003cb>request bucket\u003c/b>, the \u003cb>token bucket\u003c/b>, and a \u003cb>semaphore\u003c/b> that caps requests in flight.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Queue and prioritise.\u003c/b> Put interactive traffic ahead of background jobs, or give them separate projects and keys so a backfill cannot starve users.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Share the limiter.\u003c/b> With many server instances, a per-process bucket is not enough; use a shared limiter (for example in Redis) or a gateway such as the LiteLLM proxy (Reliability module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Ramp up gradually.\u003c/b> OpenAI returns 429 \u003ccode>slow_down\u003c/code> when traffic rises too fast even within your limits; its rule of thumb above 1 million TPM is to grow by at most 50% every 15 minutes. Anthropic describes similar acceleration limits.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Move offline work to batch APIs\u003c/b>, which have their own, larger limits (next lesson).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your limits are 1,000 RPM and 450,000 TPM, and requests average 6,000 input tokens. What throughput can you sustain, and what would raise it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>About 75 requests per minute, because 450,000 / 6,000 = 75 is far below the 1,000 RPM limit. You can raise it by shortening prompts, moving to a higher tier, splitting traffic across projects or models, or, on providers where cache reads are exempt (most Claude models), by caching the stable part of the prompt.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Two services in the same project each send half the traffic you measured as safe, yet both get 429s. Why?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Rate limits apply to the organisation or project and model, not to each service. Their combined traffic exceeds the shared limit; you need a shared limiter, separate projects, or a higher tier.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why should you set \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code> close to the expected output size on OpenAI even if you rarely hit it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The rate limiter counts a request by the larger of its estimated size and \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code>, so an oversized value reserves token headroom you never use and lowers your effective throughput.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:batch-processing-for-offline-jobs":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cb>batch API\u003c/b> takes many requests at once, runs them asynchronously within a time window (up to 24 hours), and returns all the results, at about \u003cb>half the normal price\u003c/b> and outside your normal rate limits. Anything that does not need an answer within seconds (evals, backfills, nightly classification, synthetic data) should go through it. Each request carries a \u003cb>custom id\u003c/b> so you can match results back, because results do not come back in order.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Build requests\u003c/b>\u003csmall>one per item, unique custom_id\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Submit\u003c/b>\u003csmall>upload JSONL or send a list\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Wait\u003c/b>\u003csmall>status in progress, minutes to 24 h\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Collect\u003c/b>\u003csmall>results and errors by custom_id\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Resubmit failures\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>OpenAI Batch API\u003c/th>\u003cth>Anthropic Message Batches\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Price\u003c/td>\u003ctd>50% of synchronous\u003c/td>\u003ctd>50% of standard\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Input\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Upload a \u003ccode>.jsonl\u003c/code> file: one line per request with \u003ccode>custom_id\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>method\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>url\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>body\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Send a list of \u003ccode>{custom_id, params}\u003c/code> in the create call\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Size per batch\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Up to 50,000 requests and 200 MB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Up to 100,000 requests or 256 MB\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Time window\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>completion_window: \"24h\"\u003c/code>; unfinished work is \u003ccode>expired\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Most finish within an hour; anything unfinished after 24 hours expires\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Results\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Output file plus a separate error file; line order may differ from input\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Iterate results by \u003ccode>custom_id\u003c/code>; kept for 29 days\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Rate limits\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Separate pool; does not consume your normal per-model limits\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Separate batch limits on queued requests\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Google's Gemini API offers a Batch tier at half its standard price too, and its pricing page lists a Flex tier at the same rate for requests that can tolerate delays (Billing module compares these options).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Submit 500 classification requests (OpenAI)\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import json\nimport time\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()\nSYSTEM = \"Classify the support ticket as billing, bug, account or other. Reply with the label only.\"\ntickets = load_tickets()            # 500 dicts like {\"id\": 17, \"text\": \"...\"}\n\nwith open(\"batch_input.jsonl\", \"w\") as f:\n    for t in tickets:\n        f.write(json.dumps({\n            \"custom_id\": f\"ticket-{t['id']}\",\n            \"method\": \"POST\",\n            \"url\": \"/v1/chat/completions\",\n            \"body\": {\"model\": \"&lt;model&gt;\", \"max_tokens\": 10,\n                     \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": SYSTEM},\n                                  {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": t[\"text\"]}]},\n        }) + \"\\n\")\n\nupload = client.files.create(file=open(\"batch_input.jsonl\", \"rb\"), purpose=\"batch\")\nbatch = client.batches.create(input_file_id=upload.id, endpoint=\"/v1/chat/completions\",\n                              completion_window=\"24h\")\n\nwhile (batch := client.batches.retrieve(batch.id)).status not in (\"completed\", \"failed\", \"expired\", \"cancelled\"):\n    time.sleep(60)\n\nlabels = {}\nfor line in client.files.content(batch.output_file_id).text.splitlines():\n    row = json.loads(line)\n    labels[row[\"custom_id\"]] = row[\"response\"][\"body\"][\"choices\"][0][\"message\"][\"content\"].strip()\n# Requests that failed are in batch.error_file_id: collect their custom_ids and resubmit them.\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The Anthropic version skips the file upload:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>batch = anthropic_client.messages.batches.create(requests=[\n    {\"custom_id\": f\"ticket-{t['id']}\",\n     \"params\": {\"model\": \"&lt;model&gt;\", \"max_tokens\": 10, \"system\": SYSTEM,\n                \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": t[\"text\"]}]}}\n    for t in tickets])\n# poll messages.batches.retrieve(batch.id) until processing_status == \"ended\", then:\nfor r in anthropic_client.messages.batches.results(batch.id):\n    if r.result.type == \"succeeded\":\n        labels[r.custom_id] = r.result.message.content[0].text\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The economics\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Take 500 tickets of about 250 input tokens with 3 output tokens each, at an illustrative $2 per million input and $10 per million output tokens:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Real-time: 500 × 250 × $2/M + 500 × 3 × $10/M = $0.25 + $0.015 = $0.265\nBatch:     half of that                         = $0.1325\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Trivial for 500 tickets. For a nightly job over a million documents the same arithmetic is $530 against $265, every night, for changing one endpoint.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Make jobs resumable.\u003c/b> Store the batch id as soon as you create it; a crashed script should pick up the existing job, not submit a duplicate.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Expect partial failure.\u003c/b> Some requests error or expire. Join results on \u003ccode>custom_id\u003c/code>, then resubmit only the missing ids.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Validate before submitting.\u003c/b> One malformed line can fail validation of the whole file; run a few requests synchronously first.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Combine with prompt caching\u003c/b> where supported. Anthropic notes cache hits in batches are best-effort because requests run concurrently and in any order; Billing covers the trick of warming a 1-hour cache first.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Do not use batch for users who are waiting.\u003c/b> For \"fairly soon\" work, flex tiers sit between batch and real-time (Billing module).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you submit a batch of 500 classification requests?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Build one request per ticket with a unique \u003ccode>custom_id\u003c/code>. For OpenAI, write them as JSONL lines with \u003ccode>custom_id\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>method: \"POST\"\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>url: \"/v1/chat/completions\"\u003c/code> and the normal request \u003ccode>body\u003c/code>, upload the file with \u003ccode>purpose=\"batch\"\u003c/code>, create the batch with that endpoint and \u003ccode>completion_window=\"24h\"\u003c/code>, poll until it completes, then download the output file and map each line back by \u003ccode>custom_id\u003c/code>, resubmitting anything in the error file. For Anthropic, pass a list of \u003ccode>{custom_id, params}\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>messages.batches.create\u003c/code>, poll until \u003ccode>processing_status\u003c/code> is \u003ccode>ended\u003c/code>, and iterate the results.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why must you match batch results by custom id instead of by position?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The output order is not guaranteed to match the input order, and failed or expired requests are missing from the main output, so positions do not line up.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name three workloads that should use a batch API and one that should not.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Should: running evals over a test set, classifying or enriching a large backlog, generating embeddings or synthetic data overnight. Should not: anything a user is actively waiting for, such as a chat reply, because results can take up to 24 hours.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:apis/provider-request-shapes-compared":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>The big three APIs carry the same ideas (a system prompt, alternating turns, an output cap, tools, usage) in different field names and nesting. The differences that bite are \u003cb>where the system prompt goes\u003c/b>, \u003cb>whether the output cap is required\u003c/b>, \u003cb>how tool calls and results are represented\u003c/b>, and \u003cb>what the usage numbers mean\u003c/b>. Knowing them lets you write adapters, debug gateways and read any provider's logs.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>One request, three dialects\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The same request (a system prompt, one user message, a 500-token cap) in each native format:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>POST https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions\nAuthorization: Bearer $OPENAI_API_KEY\n\n{\"model\": \"&lt;model&gt;\",\n \"max_completion_tokens\": 500,\n \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"developer\", \"content\": \"You are a terse assistant.\"},\n              {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Name three sorting algorithms.\"}]}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>POST https://api.anthropic.com/v1/messages\nx-api-key: $ANTHROPIC_API_KEY\nanthropic-version: 2023-06-01\n\n{\"model\": \"&lt;model&gt;\",\n \"max_tokens\": 500,\n \"system\": \"You are a terse assistant.\",\n \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Name three sorting algorithms.\"}]}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>POST https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/models/&lt;model&gt;:generateContent\nx-goog-api-key: $GEMINI_API_KEY\n\n{\"systemInstruction\": {\"parts\": [{\"text\": \"You are a terse assistant.\"}]},\n \"contents\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"parts\": [{\"text\": \"Name three sorting algorithms.\"}]}],\n \"generationConfig\": {\"maxOutputTokens\": 500}}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Side by side\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>OpenAI Chat Completions\u003c/th>\u003cth>Anthropic Messages\u003c/th>\u003cth>Gemini generateContent\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Auth\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>Authorization: Bearer\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>x-api-key\u003c/code> plus \u003ccode>anthropic-version\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>x-goog-api-key\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>System prompt\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A \u003ccode>developer\u003c/code> (or \u003ccode>system\u003c/code>) message\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Top-level \u003ccode>system\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Top-level \u003ccode>systemInstruction\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Model's role name\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>assistant\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>assistant\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>model\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Content\u003c/td>\u003ctd>String or list of parts\u003c/td>\u003ctd>String or list of content blocks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>List of \u003ccode>parts\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Output cap\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>max_completion_tokens\u003c/code>, optional\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code>, \u003cb>required\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>generationConfig.maxOutputTokens\u003c/code>, optional\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Tool definitions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>{\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {name, description, parameters}}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>{name, description, input_schema}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>{\"functionDeclarations\": [...]}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Model calls a tool\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>message.tool_calls[]\u003c/code>, arguments as a JSON \u003cb>string\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>tool_use\u003c/code> content block, \u003ccode>input\u003c/code> as an \u003cb>object\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>functionCall\u003c/code> part, \u003ccode>args\u003c/code> as an object\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>You return the result\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Message with role \u003ccode>tool\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>tool_call_id\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>tool_result\u003c/code> block inside a \u003cb>user\u003c/b> message\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>functionResponse\u003c/code> part\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Why it stopped\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>finish_reason\u003c/code>: \u003ccode>stop\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>length\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>tool_calls\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>stop_reason\u003c/code>: \u003ccode>end_turn\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>tool_use\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>refusal\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>finishReason\u003c/code>: \u003ccode>STOP\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>MAX_TOKENS\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Input tokens\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>usage.prompt_tokens\u003c/code> (includes cached)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>usage.input_tokens\u003c/code> (only the part \u003cb>not\u003c/b> read from or written to cache)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>usageMetadata.promptTokenCount\u003c/code> (includes cached)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Output tokens\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>usage.completion_tokens\u003c/code> (includes reasoning)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>usage.output_tokens\u003c/code> (includes thinking)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>candidatesTokenCount\u003c/code>, plus \u003ccode>thoughtsTokenCount\u003c/code> for thinking\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Cache fields\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>prompt_tokens_details.cached_tokens\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>cache_read_input_tokens\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>cache_creation_input_tokens\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>cachedContentTokenCount\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The usage row is the most dangerous. If you compute cost from Anthropic's \u003ccode>input_tokens\u003c/code> alone you will undercount heavily on cached workloads, because the cached part is reported in separate fields. If you add OpenAI's \u003ccode>cached_tokens\u003c/code> on top of \u003ccode>prompt_tokens\u003c/code> you will double-count. The Billing module builds a cost function that normalises all of this.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Newer native APIs\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each provider also has a richer, newer API alongside the classic one: OpenAI's \u003cb>Responses API\u003c/b> (\u003ccode>instructions\u003c/code> plus \u003ccode>input\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>max_output_tokens\u003c/code>, a list of typed output items, usage as \u003ccode>input_tokens\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>output_tokens\u003c/code>) and Google's \u003cb>Interactions API\u003c/b> (\u003ccode>input\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>system_instruction\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>generation_config\u003c/code>, and usage fields such as \u003ccode>total_input_tokens\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>total_cached_tokens\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>total_thought_tokens\u003c/code>, as of late 2026). They add server-side state and built-in tools, but the concepts in the table carry over one to one.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Compatibility endpoints\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Most providers now accept the OpenAI format directly: Gemini at \u003ccode>https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/openai/\u003c/code>, Anthropic at \u003ccode>https://api.anthropic.com/v1/\u003c/code>, and inference hosts and local servers too. Read the fine print. Anthropic describes its compatibility layer as meant for testing and comparing models, not as a long-term production path, and lists what it drops: prompt caching is not supported, the \u003ccode>strict\u003c/code> flag for tool schemas is ignored, \u003ccode>response_format\u003c/code> is ignored, and most unsupported fields are \u003cb>silently ignored\u003c/b> rather than rejected.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use the OpenAI-compatible format for quick comparisons and the common path; use native SDKs where you need provider features (caching controls, thinking settings, citations, PDFs).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Hide the differences in a small adapter layer with a normalised result type: text, tool calls, stop reason, and usage split into uncached input, cache reads, cache writes and output.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write a tiny \u003cb>contract test\u003c/b> per provider (one plain call, one tool call, one streamed call) and run it in CI; formats drift.\u003c/li>\u003cli>When something odd happens behind a gateway, log the actual outbound request. Silent translation is the usual culprit.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Where does the system prompt go in each of the three native APIs?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>OpenAI Chat Completions: a message with role \u003ccode>developer\u003c/code> (or \u003ccode>system\u003c/code> on older models) in \u003ccode>messages\u003c/code>. Anthropic Messages: the top-level \u003ccode>system\u003c/code> field. Gemini generateContent: the top-level \u003ccode>systemInstruction\u003c/code> field with text parts.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your cost report shows Claude as ten times cheaper than expected on a chat feature with prompt caching. What is the likely bug?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The cost was computed from \u003ccode>usage.input_tokens\u003c/code> only. In Anthropic's API that field excludes tokens read from or written to the cache, which are reported in \u003ccode>cache_read_input_tokens\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>cache_creation_input_tokens\u003c/code> and must be priced separately.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name three things that can be lost when you call Claude through an OpenAI-compatible endpoint.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Prompt caching (not supported there), strict tool-schema enforcement (\u003ccode>strict\u003c/code> is ignored), \u003ccode>response_format\u003c/code> JSON mode (ignored), plus provider features such as thinking controls and citations; unsupported fields are often silently ignored.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:one-client-for-many-providers":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>There are three ways to make your code provider-agnostic: point the \u003cb>OpenAI SDK at compatible endpoints\u003c/b>, use a \u003cb>translation library or proxy\u003c/b> such as LiteLLM, or write \u003cb>your own thin adapter interface\u003c/b> over native SDKs. Teams usually combine them: a common path for most calls, native adapters for provider-specific features, and contract tests to catch drift.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Option 1: OpenAI-compatible endpoints\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Many providers, inference hosts and local servers speak the Chat Completions format, so switching is a matter of base URL, key and model name:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import os\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nBACKENDS = {\n    \"openai\": OpenAI(),\n    \"gemini\": OpenAI(base_url=\"https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/openai/\",\n                     api_key=os.environ[\"GEMINI_API_KEY\"]),\n    \"local\":  OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:11434/v1\", api_key=\"ollama\"),\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Cheapest option to build; weakest on provider features (see the previous lesson's compatibility caveats).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Option 2: a translation library or proxy\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>LiteLLM\u003c/b> translates one OpenAI-style call into each provider's native API and back, and normalises the response, usage and errors. The provider is a prefix on the model string:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from litellm import completion\n\nmessages = [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"Answer in one sentence.\"},\n            {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"What is a race condition?\"}]\n\n# Keys come from OPENAI_API_KEY, ANTHROPIC_API_KEY and GEMINI_API_KEY\nfor model in [\"openai/&lt;model&gt;\", \"anthropic/&lt;model&gt;\", \"gemini/&lt;model&gt;\"]:\n    r = completion(model=model, messages=messages, max_tokens=100, timeout=30)\n    print(model, \"|\", r.choices[0].message.content)\n    print(\"   tokens:\", r.usage.prompt_tokens, \"in,\", r.usage.completion_tokens, \"out;\",\n          \"cost $\", r._hidden_params.get(\"response_cost\"))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>LiteLLM also runs as a \u003cb>proxy server\u003c/b>: a service your whole organisation calls with the plain OpenAI SDK, which adds virtual keys, per-team budgets, fallbacks, logging and caching. Other libraries in this space: the Vercel AI SDK (TypeScript), LangChain's chat model interface and Pydantic AI's model classes. Hosted routers such as OpenRouter do the same as a service.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Option 3: your own adapter interface\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A small interface you own keeps the rest of the code base independent of any vendor:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import Protocol\n\n@dataclass\nclass Usage:\n    input_total: int        # all input tokens, including cache reads and writes\n    cache_read: int\n    cache_write: int\n    output: int             # includes reasoning tokens\n\n@dataclass\nclass Result:\n    text: str\n    tool_calls: list\n    stop_reason: str        # normalised: \"end\", \"length\", \"tool\", \"refusal\"\n    usage: Usage\n    model: str\n    request_id: str\n\nclass LLM(Protocol):\n    def generate(self, messages: list[dict], *, max_tokens: int, **opts) -&gt; Result: ...\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Each adapter (OpenAI-compatible, Anthropic native, Gemini native) maps its response into \u003ccode>Result\u003c/code>. Behind the adapter you may still use LiteLLM or raw HTTP. This is what you build in the Atlas M1 milestone at the end of this module.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What gets lost in a common format\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Feature\u003c/th>\u003cth>What can go wrong\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Prompt caching\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Cache-control markers dropped or unsupported; cached-token counts missing from usage\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Structured output\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Strict schema enforcement ignored by some compatibility layers\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Reasoning settings\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Effort levels, thinking budgets and thinking levels have different names and ranges\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Usage details\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Reasoning and cache fields renamed, merged or absent, so costs are wrong\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Provider tools\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Web search, code execution, citations, PDF input may not translate\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Errors\u003c/td>\u003ctd>529 versus 503, retry headers and error codes flattened into generic exceptions\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Silent ignoring\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Unsupported parameters accepted without error and without effect\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The rule of thumb: \u003cb>test every feature you depend on through the layer you actually use\u003c/b>, and log the outbound request when results surprise you.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Keep model choice in \u003cb>configuration\u003c/b> (an alias such as \u003ccode>fast\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>smart\u003c/code> mapped to provider and model), so switching is a config change plus an eval run.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Store \u003cb>prices next to the alias\u003c/b> so cost logging does not depend on a library's price table being current.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A gateway is also a data processor: check its logging and retention, especially hosted routers.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Avoid over-abstraction. A 200-line adapter you understand beats a framework you have to debug.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you call three providers through LiteLLM with the same code?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Install \u003ccode>litellm\u003c/code>, set each provider's key in the environment (for example \u003ccode>OPENAI_API_KEY\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>GEMINI_API_KEY\u003c/code>), and call \u003ccode>litellm.completion(model=..., messages=...)\u003c/code> in a loop with provider-prefixed model names such as \u003ccode>openai/&lt;model&gt;\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>anthropic/&lt;model&gt;\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>gemini/&lt;model&gt;\u003c/code>, passing identical messages. Read the answer from \u003ccode>r.choices[0].message.content\u003c/code>, tokens from \u003ccode>r.usage\u003c/code>, and the estimated cost from \u003ccode>r._hidden_params[\"response_cost\"]\u003c/code>. For a team, run the LiteLLM proxy and point the ordinary OpenAI SDK at it.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">When would you write native adapters instead of relying only on an OpenAI-compatible endpoint?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When you need provider-specific features that the common format drops or mistranslates: prompt-caching controls, thinking or effort settings, strict structured outputs, citations, document inputs, or accurate cache and reasoning token counts for billing.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why keep prices in your own model configuration?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Prices change often and differ by tier, region and caching. Your cost logs and budgets should not depend on a third-party price table being up to date, and you need to record which price was used.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:running-models-locally":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Local inference servers expose the same OpenAI-compatible API as cloud providers, so your client code does not change: only the base URL and model name do. That makes local models useful for development without a bill, tests in CI, private data that must not leave a machine, and cheap high-volume jobs, as long as you respect what a small model can and cannot do.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The servers\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Tool\u003c/th>\u003cth>Best for\u003c/th>\u003cth>Start it\u003c/th>\u003cth>Base URL\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Ollama\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One-command local models on a laptop or server\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ollama pull &lt;model&gt;\u003c/code> then the app or \u003ccode>ollama serve\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>http://localhost:11434/v1\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>LM Studio\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Desktop app with a chat UI and a local server\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Load a model, then start the server in the app or with \u003ccode>lms server start\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>http://localhost:1234/v1\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>llama.cpp \u003ccode>llama-server\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Minimal, fast engine for GGUF files, CPU or GPU\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>llama-server -m model.gguf\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>http://localhost:8080/v1\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>vLLM\u003c/td>\u003ctd>GPU serving for many concurrent users\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>vllm serve &lt;hugging-face-model-id&gt;\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>http://localhost:8000/v1\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Ollama, LM Studio and llama.cpp run quantised GGUF models well on laptops; vLLM (and SGLang) are built for throughput on GPU servers with continuous batching (Serving module).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Switch with one setting\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import os\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(\n    base_url=os.getenv(\"LLM_BASE_URL\", \"http://localhost:11434/v1\"),\n    api_key=os.getenv(\"LLM_API_KEY\", \"ollama\"),   # local servers need a value but ignore it\n)\nr = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=os.getenv(\"LLM_MODEL\", \"&lt;local-model&gt;\"),\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Summarise what a mutex is in one sentence.\"}],\n    max_tokens=100,\n)\nprint(r.choices[0].message.content, r.usage)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Run the same script with \u003ccode>LLM_BASE_URL\u003c/code> pointing at a cloud provider and it calls the cloud model instead. This is the property your Atlas client builds on.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What changes when you go local\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Context length.\u003c/b> Local servers often load a model with a context window smaller than its maximum to save memory. Ollama lets you raise the default with the \u003ccode>OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH\u003c/code> environment variable (or per request); otherwise long prompts may be silently truncated.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Model names.\u003c/b> Use the local name or tag (for example a name plus a quantisation tag), not the cloud model id.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Feature support.\u003c/b> Basic chat and streaming are solid. Tool calling, structured output and vision depend on the model and server; test them.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Usage and cost.\u003c/b> Usage counts are reported, but cost is your hardware and electricity, not per token. Log tokens anyway; you need them for capacity planning.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Speed.\u003c/b> A laptop gives you tens of tokens per second for a small quantised model, with TTFT that grows with prompt length. Fine for development and batch work, often too slow for many concurrent users.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Quality.\u003c/b> Small models are good at narrow tasks (classification, extraction, short summaries) and weaker at long multi-step reasoning (Landscape module).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Tests and CI:\u003c/b> point integration tests at a small local model, so they run without keys or cost. Keep a few tests against the real provider too.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Privacy tiers:\u003c/b> route requests containing sensitive data to a local or self-hosted model and the rest to a frontier API.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cascades:\u003c/b> try a local model first and escalate to a cloud model when a check fails (Reliability module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Do not expose the port.\u003c/b> Local servers usually have no authentication by default; bind them to localhost or put an authenticating proxy in front before opening them to a network.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you serve a local model with Ollama and call its OpenAI-compatible endpoint?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Install Ollama, run \u003ccode>ollama pull &lt;model&gt;\u003c/code> (and \u003ccode>ollama serve\u003c/code> if the app is not already running), then create the OpenAI client with \u003ccode>base_url=\"http://localhost:11434/v1\"\u003c/code> and any non-empty \u003ccode>api_key\u003c/code>, and call \u003ccode>client.chat.completions.create(model=\"&lt;model&gt;\", messages=[...])\u003c/code> exactly as you would for a cloud model.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A long document summarised by your local model ignores its first half. What should you check first?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The context window the server loaded the model with. Local servers often default to a smaller window than the model supports, so the prompt is truncated; raise it (for Ollama, \u003ccode>OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH\u003c/code> or a per-request option) within your memory limits.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Give two good reasons to keep a local model in your stack even if production uses a cloud API.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Any two of: free and offline development and CI tests; data that must not leave your machine or network; a cheap first step in a cascade or for high-volume simple tasks; a fallback when the provider is down.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:keeping-keys-and-data-safe":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>An API key is both a credit card and a door to your data. Keep keys out of code and clients, give each service its own \u003cb>scoped\u003c/b> key, cap spending with \u003cb>limits and alerts\u003c/b>, and make sure neither keys nor users' personal data end up in logs. Then check what the provider itself stores: by default business API data is not used for training, but it may be retained for a period for abuse monitoring, and stored conversation state is retained longer.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Where keys live\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Environment\u003c/th>\u003cth>Do\u003c/th>\u003cth>Do not\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Laptop\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A \u003ccode>.env\u003c/code> file listed in \u003ccode>.gitignore\u003c/code>, loaded at start-up\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Paste keys into notebooks or source files\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>CI\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The CI system's encrypted secrets\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Print environment variables in build logs\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Production\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A secret manager (AWS Secrets Manager, Google Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault, HashiCorp Vault) injected at runtime\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Bake keys into container images\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Browser or mobile app\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Call your own backend, which holds the key\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Ship a provider key in client code: anyone can extract it\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Bots scan public repositories continuously for leaked keys, so a key pushed to a public repo should be treated as compromised at once: revoke it, then clean the history. GitHub's secret scanning and push protection catch many provider key formats before they land; turn them on.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Scope, rotate and cap\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>One key per service and environment.\u003c/b> Use OpenAI \u003cb>projects\u003c/b> or Anthropic \u003cb>workspaces\u003c/b> to separate production, staging and experiments; each gets its own keys, limits and usage reports. A leaked staging key then cannot touch production.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Least privilege.\u003c/b> Where the platform supports it, restrict keys to the endpoints or models they need, and use IP allowlists for server-side keys.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Rotate\u003c/b> on a schedule and immediately when someone leaves or a key appears anywhere it should not.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Spend limits and alerts.\u003c/b> Set hard monthly limits per project or workspace, plus alerts at, say, 50% and 80%. When OpenAI's hard limit is reached, requests fail with a 429 that names the limit; Anthropic returns an error when a workspace spend limit you set is reached. A runaway agent loop then costs a known maximum.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Load keys and redact logs\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import logging\nimport os\nimport re\n\nfrom dotenv import load_dotenv   # pip install python-dotenv; for local development only\n\nload_dotenv()                                 # reads .env if present\nOPENAI_API_KEY = os.environ[\"OPENAI_API_KEY\"] # KeyError at start-up beats a confusing 401 later\n\nPATTERNS = [\n    (re.compile(r\"\\bsk-[A-Za-z0-9_-]{16,}\"), \"[REDACTED_KEY]\"),     # OpenAI and Anthropic style keys\n    (re.compile(r\"\\bAIza[0-9A-Za-z_-]{35}\\b\"), \"[REDACTED_KEY]\"),   # Google API keys\n    (re.compile(r\"[\\w.+-]+@[\\w-]+\\.[\\w.-]+\"), \"[EMAIL]\"),\n    (re.compile(r\"\\b(?:\\d[ -]?){13,16}\\b\"), \"[CARD]\"),\n]\n\nclass RedactFilter(logging.Filter):\n    def filter(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -&gt; bool:\n        text = record.getMessage()\n        for pattern, replacement in PATTERNS:\n            text = pattern.sub(replacement, text)\n        record.msg, record.args = text, None\n        return True\n\nlog = logging.getLogger(\"atlas.llm\")\nlog.addFilter(RedactFilter())\nlog.info(\"user %s asked: %s\", \"jane.doe@example.com\", \"my card 4111 1111 1111 1111 was charged twice\")\n# -&gt; user [EMAIL] asked: my card [CARD] was charged twice\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Regex redaction is a safety net, not a guarantee. The stronger habits: never log request headers (they contain the key), log \u003cb>metadata by default\u003c/b> (model, token counts, cost, latency, ids) and full prompts and responses only where you have a reason, a retention period and access control. Observability tools such as Langfuse offer masking hooks for the same purpose (Observability module).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What the provider keeps\u003c/h4>\u003cp>As of late 2026, the major providers' business APIs do not train on your data by default (OpenAI states this has been the case since 1 March 2023). Retention is a separate question:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Data\u003c/th>\u003cth>Typical default\u003c/th>\u003cth>Controls\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Abuse-monitoring logs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>OpenAI: retained for up to 30 days\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Zero Data Retention or Modified Abuse Monitoring, for approved customers\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Stored responses and conversations\u003c/td>\u003ctd>OpenAI Responses: 30 days when \u003ccode>store=true\u003c/code>; Conversations: until deleted\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>store=false\u003c/code>; delete via the API\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Prompt-cache entries\u003c/td>\u003ctd>In memory, minutes to hours (Billing module)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Isolated per organisation (Anthropic: per workspace); compatible with zero data retention\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Free tiers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Google's Gemini pricing page says free-tier content is used to improve its products; paid-tier content is not\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Use a paid tier for anything sensitive\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Also check \u003cb>region\u003c/b> (where data is processed; several providers sell regional processing at a premium) and sign a \u003cb>data processing agreement\u003c/b> before sending customer personal data.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Minimise.\u003c/b> Do not send fields the model does not need; replace names, emails and account numbers with placeholders before the call when the task allows it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Separate keys per tenant or feature\u003c/b> if you need per-customer cost reports or the ability to cut one off.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Audit third parties in the path.\u003c/b> Gateways, observability tools and hosted routers all see prompts; each needs the same scrutiny as the model provider.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Treat prompts and outputs that contain user data as \u003cb>user data\u003c/b>: they fall under deletion requests and retention policies (Governance module).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you load keys from the environment and redact logs?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Keep keys in environment variables, loaded from a git-ignored \u003ccode>.env\u003c/code> file locally (for example with python-dotenv) and from a secret manager in production, and read them with \u003ccode>os.environ[\"...\"]\u003c/code> so a missing key fails at start-up. Attach a logging filter that replaces key patterns, emails and card-like numbers with placeholders, never log request headers, and log metadata rather than full prompts by default.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A developer wants to call the model directly from the company's mobile app to save a server hop. What do you say?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>No: any key shipped in client code can be extracted and abused. The app should call your backend, which authenticates the user, applies limits and holds the provider key (or issues short-lived tokens where the provider supports them).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name three controls that limit the damage of a leaked or misused key.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Separate keys per service and environment (projects or workspaces), hard spend limits with alerts, and least-privilege restrictions such as allowed endpoints or IP allowlists, together with quick rotation and revocation.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","lab:apis/a-streaming-client-with-retries-and-timing":"\u003cp>You will write a small client that streams an answer, retries transient failures before the first token, and reports TTFT, total time and output speed. Then you will test it against a deliberately flaky local server, so you can see each failure path without waiting for a real outage.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Python 3.11+, \u003ccode>pip install openai httpx\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\u003cli>One real endpoint: a provider key, or Ollama with a small model (\u003ccode>http://localhost:11434/v1\u003c/code>)\u003c/li>\u003cli>Two terminals\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Save this flaky server as \u003ccode>flaky_server.py\u003c/code> and start it with \u003ccode>python flaky_server.py\u003c/code>. It answers 429 twice, then streams a reply in Chat Completions format:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import json\nimport time\nfrom http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, HTTPServer\n\ncalls = 0\n\nclass Handler(BaseHTTPRequestHandler):\n    def do_POST(self):\n        global calls\n        calls += 1\n        self.rfile.read(int(self.headers.get(\"Content-Length\", 0)))\n        if calls &lt;= 2:                                   # first two calls: rate limited\n            body = json.dumps({\"error\": {\"message\": \"slow down\", \"type\": \"rate_limit_error\"}}).encode()\n            self.send_response(429)\n            self.send_header(\"Retry-After\", \"1\")\n            self.send_header(\"Content-Type\", \"application/json\")\n            self.send_header(\"Content-Length\", str(len(body)))\n            self.end_headers()\n            self.wfile.write(body)\n            return\n        self.send_response(200)\n        self.send_header(\"Content-Type\", \"text/event-stream\")\n        self.end_headers()\n        time.sleep(0.5)                                  # pretend prefill\n        for word in \"Streaming works and so do retries.\".split():\n            chunk = {\"id\": \"mock\", \"object\": \"chat.completion.chunk\", \"created\": 0, \"model\": \"mock\",\n                     \"choices\": [{\"index\": 0, \"delta\": {\"content\": word + \" \"}, \"finish_reason\": None}]}\n            self.wfile.write(f\"data: {json.dumps(chunk)}\\n\\n\".encode())\n            self.wfile.flush()\n            time.sleep(0.1)\n        usage = {\"id\": \"mock\", \"object\": \"chat.completion.chunk\", \"created\": 0, \"model\": \"mock\",\n                 \"choices\": [], \"usage\": {\"prompt_tokens\": 12, \"completion_tokens\": 6, \"total_tokens\": 18}}\n        self.wfile.write(f\"data: {json.dumps(usage)}\\n\\n\".encode())\n        self.wfile.write(b\"data: [DONE]\\n\\n\")\n\n    def log_message(self, *args):\n        pass\n\nHTTPServer((\"localhost\", 8099), Handler).serve_forever()\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"2\">\u003cli>Save the client as \u003ccode>stream_client.py\u003c/code>. Configuration comes from environment variables so the same file works for the mock, a cloud model and a local model:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import os\nimport random\nimport time\n\nimport httpx\nimport openai\n\nclient = openai.OpenAI(base_url=os.getenv(\"LLM_BASE_URL\"), api_key=os.getenv(\"LLM_API_KEY\", \"none\"),\n                       timeout=httpx.Timeout(60.0, connect=5.0), max_retries=0)\nMODEL = os.getenv(\"LLM_MODEL\", \"mock\")\nRETRYABLE = (openai.APIConnectionError, openai.RateLimitError, openai.InternalServerError)\n\nclass PartialStream(Exception):\n    \"\"\"The stream broke after text was shown: do not retry silently.\"\"\"\n\ndef stream_once(messages):\n    t0 = time.perf_counter()\n    ttft, parts, usage = None, [], None\n    stream = client.chat.completions.create(model=MODEL, messages=messages, max_tokens=400,\n                                            stream=True, stream_options={\"include_usage\": True})\n    try:\n        for chunk in stream:\n            if chunk.choices and chunk.choices[0].delta.content:\n                if ttft is None:\n                    ttft = time.perf_counter() - t0\n                parts.append(chunk.choices[0].delta.content)\n                print(chunk.choices[0].delta.content, end=\"\", flush=True)\n            if chunk.usage:\n                usage = chunk.usage\n    except Exception as e:\n        if parts:\n            raise PartialStream(\"\".join(parts)) from e\n        raise\n    return \"\".join(parts), ttft, time.perf_counter() - t0, usage\n\ndef retry_after(e):\n    headers = getattr(getattr(e, \"response\", None), \"headers\", None) or {}\n    try:\n        return float(headers.get(\"retry-after\", 0))\n    except ValueError:\n        return 0.0\n\ndef stream_with_retries(messages, max_attempts=5, base=1.0, cap=20.0):\n    for attempt in range(1, max_attempts + 1):\n        try:\n            text, ttft, total, usage = stream_once(messages)\n            return dict(text=text, ttft=ttft, total=total, usage=usage, attempts=attempt)\n        except RETRYABLE as e:\n            if attempt == max_attempts:\n                raise\n            delay = max(random.uniform(0, min(cap, base * 2 ** (attempt - 1))), retry_after(e))\n            print(f\"[attempt {attempt}: {type(e).__name__}, retrying in {delay:.1f}s]\")\n            time.sleep(delay)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    r = stream_with_retries([{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Explain what an API rate limit is in 120 words.\"}])\n    u = r[\"usage\"]\n    print(f\"\\nattempts={r['attempts']} ttft={r['ttft']:.2f}s total={r['total']:.2f}s \"\n          f\"in={u.prompt_tokens} out={u.completion_tokens} \"\n          f\"speed={u.completion_tokens / (r['total'] - r['ttft']):.1f} tok/s\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"3\">\u003cli>Run it against the mock: \u003ccode>LLM_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8099/v1 python stream_client.py\u003c/code>. You should see two retry lines, then the streamed sentence and the timing line. Restart the server to reset its counter.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Break it on purpose and note what each failure looks like:\u003cul>\u003cli>Stop the server and run again: \u003ccode>APIConnectionError\u003c/code>, retried, then raised after five attempts.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Change \u003ccode>connect=5.0\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>connect=0.001\u003c/code> and point at a remote provider: a connect timeout.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Change \u003ccode>calls &lt;= 2\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>calls &lt;= 10\u003c/code> in the server: the client gives up after five attempts.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/li>\u003cli>Point it at a real model (a provider or Ollama) by setting \u003ccode>LLM_BASE_URL\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>LLM_API_KEY\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>LLM_MODEL\u003c/code>. Run it five times and write down TTFT, total time and tokens per second for each run.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Make the prompt ten times longer (paste a long document before the question) and run five more times. Compare TTFT.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Against the mock: \u003ccode>attempts=3\u003c/code>, a TTFT of about 0.5 s and a total of about 1.1 s.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Against a real model: TTFT from a few hundred milliseconds to a few seconds, varying run to run; output speed fairly stable for a given model.\u003c/li>\u003cli>With the long prompt: TTFT rises noticeably (more prefill work) while output speed barely changes, which is exactly the prefill versus decode split from the Inference module.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does the client refuse to retry once some text has been printed?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A retry generates a fresh, probably different answer, so the user would see text that does not join up. Once output has been shown, the right move is to surface the interruption and let the user (or explicit continuation logic) decide.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is TTFT more sensitive to prompt length than output speed is?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The whole prompt must be processed (prefill) before the first token appears, so longer prompts delay it; after that, each output token costs roughly the same decode step regardless of how the prompt began.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","b:provider-agnostic-llm-client":"\u003ch4>The brief\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Atlas is the AI assistant you grow through this guide: it will answer questions over a document collection you care about and take actions through tools. Every later milestone sends its model calls through the module you build now, \u003ccode>atlas/llm.py\u003c/code>. \u003cb>Starting point:\u003c/b> an empty repository. \u003cb>This milestone adds:\u003c/b> one small client that can talk to two cloud providers and a local model through one interface, streams, survives transient failures, and writes one structured log line per call with tokens, cost and latency.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Requirements\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A model configuration file (for example \u003ccode>models.toml\u003c/code>) mapping \u003cb>aliases\u003c/b> such as \u003ccode>fast\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>smart\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>local\u003c/code> to provider, model id, base URL and prices per million tokens (input, cached input, cache write, output).\u003c/li>\u003cli>One interface, for example \u003ccode>generate(messages, *, model=\"smart\", max_tokens=..., **opts) -&gt; Result\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>stream(...)\u003c/code> yielding text deltas and ending with a \u003ccode>Result\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Two adapters: an \u003cb>OpenAI-compatible\u003c/b> adapter (covers OpenAI, Gemini's compatibility endpoint, Ollama, vLLM) and a \u003cb>native Anthropic\u003c/b> adapter.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A normalised \u003ccode>Usage\u003c/code> type: total input, cache reads, cache writes, output (including reasoning), so later milestones can price any provider the same way.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Timeouts per phase (connect, read, overall deadline) and retries with exponential backoff, full jitter and \u003ccode>Retry-After\u003c/code>; never retry 400, 401, 403, 404 or billing errors; never retry automatically after streamed text has been shown.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A JSON log line per call: timestamp, request id, alias, provider, model, prompt version (a string you pass in), input, cached, cache-write and output tokens, cost in USD, TTFT, total time, attempts, status and error type. Keys and personal data must not appear.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keys from environment variables or a secret manager only.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Milestones\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Repository skeleton, \u003ccode>models.toml\u003c/code>, the \u003ccode>Result\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>Usage\u003c/code> types, and a \u003ccode>python -m atlas.chat --model &lt;alias&gt;\u003c/code> REPL that keeps conversation history.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The OpenAI-compatible adapter, non-streaming, with usage normalisation. Test it against a cloud model and Ollama.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Streaming with TTFT measurement, including the final usage chunk.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Timeouts and the retry policy. Write pytest tests against the flaky server from the lab (or a mocking library such as \u003ccode>respx\u003c/code>): retries on 429 and 503, no retry on 400 and 401, deadline respected.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The native Anthropic adapter (streaming and non-streaming), mapping \u003ccode>cache_read_input_tokens\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>cache_creation_input_tokens\u003c/code> into \u003ccode>Usage\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Cost calculation from the configured prices and the JSON log line, with the redaction filter.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A comparison run: ten prompts through all three aliases, producing a small table of quality notes, TTFT, total time and cost per request in the README.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Done when\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>The same chat script runs against two cloud providers and a local model by changing only \u003ccode>--model\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Every call writes one JSON log line with tokens, cost and latency, and no secret ever appears in logs or the repository.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The test suite proves the retry and timeout policy without calling a real API.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The README explains how to add a new provider in under ten lines of configuration or code.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Stretch goals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>An async version with the token-bucket limiter and a concurrency semaphore from the rate-limits lesson.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A fallback chain: if the primary alias fails after retries, try a secondary one, and log that it happened.\u003c/li>\u003cli>OpenTelemetry spans per call using the GenAI semantic-convention attribute names (for example \u003ccode>gen_ai.usage.input_tokens\u003c/code>), ready for the Observability module.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A LiteLLM-backed adapter as a third implementation of the same interface, to compare effort and behaviour.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Hints\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Keep the adapters dumb and the policy (retries, timeouts, logging) in one wrapper around them, so every provider gets the same behaviour.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Record the price you used in each log line; prices change, and old logs must stay explainable.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use \u003ccode>time.perf_counter()\u003c/code> for durations and log milliseconds as integers.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>"}]);
